course: dictionary
lesson: 7881
title: Ramification: results and branches
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A ramification is a result that follows from something you do or decide, and it is often more complicated than you expected. If you skip practice, the ramifications could include missed skills, a worse score and extra work to catch up. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in exams, news reports and grown-up writing.
explain: «The Mole is staggered at the size, the extent, the ramifications of it all.» Here ramifications means branches: passages and rooms that spread off in more and more directions, like the branches of a tree. The clues are size and extent, so the word must name something with many spreading parts.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. "The Mole is staggered at the size, the extent, the branches of it all" still makes sense, so the branch meaning fits. For a decision, try "the results of the choice" in place of ramifications. If the sentence still works, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Ramification is a formal noun, and it is usually used in the plural: ramifications.
fact: A ramification can be a result or consequence of a decision, often one that is complicated or not expected.
fact: A ramification can also be a branch, or one of many branches, of something like a tree or river.
fact: «Ramifications» in the Mole's sentence means passages, store-chambers, arches and pavements branching off in many directions.
fact: Context clues help: if the sentence talks about a choice and its effects, think result; if it talks about spreading parts, think branch.
exercise: Ben thinks about the ramifications of skipping practice. What does ramifications mean here? | A map of the school | *The results that follow from his choice | A loud noise made by a drum | The branches of a tree
exercise: «The Mole is staggered at the size, the extent, the ramifications of it all.» Which word in the sentence is the best clue to the meaning of ramifications? | *extent | Mole | staggered | all
exercise: The council must think about the ramifications of the new law. Which meaning fits best? | A quick and simple answer | The colour of the council's signs | *The results and effects the law will have | The rooms inside a building
test: The team did not think about the ramifications of changing the rules. Which word is closest in meaning? | pauses | sounds | *consequences | decorations
test: The river has many ramifications across the valley, each one a smaller stream. What does ramifications mean here? | rules | *branches | ideas | injuries
exam: A formal report warns that the ramifications of the plan are complicated and may be unexpected. What does ramifications mean? | the plan's first step | *results or consequences, often complicated | a type of stone | a set of musical notes
